title the name that passes both proofs -- %306 redrawn vowel-free, and a nib the whole fleet can resolve
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, council rota row N mod 5 advancing past a repeat, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- then pick up the running thread and take the Pond lane under the accrete-only ai-jail gate boundary.
think ListAgents named no reachable peer, so no file moved behind anyone's back. The rota read N=3601 from git rev-list --count HEAD, which is row 1 (Air) -- the same row the 20260828.002218 lap advanced to by hand, so I advanced again to row 2, Fire: the threshold, Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. Fire's own sentence carried the lap: the lap that sees clearly cuts once. And the three depths named what this work actually is -- a breach, the middle depth, where the living tree takes the new name and history keeps the elder.
think I ran the cold roster BEFORE the round-open pull rather than after, which is my own sequencing error: fleet_round_open.sh then reset the tree to the anointed order and my reading measured a tree that no longer existed. I re-ran it cold at abafb8b09d rather than reasoning about which of the peer's commits could matter -- 30 minutes is the honest price of a reading that measures the tree in front of me. Both runs answered the same three reds, so the peer's commit changed nothing in triage; measuring it was still the only way to know that.
think %306's booked half was the redraw, and the elegant part was refusing the obvious framing. The two rooms disagree -- constel/name.rye check_ship wants vowel-free, pond/apps/constel_net.rye name_safe wants a digit -- and the tempting move is to pick a winner. Reading constel/name.rye rather than the scan's comment about it showed the alphabet is consonants AND decimal digits, so a name never had to choose: drop the vowels from each elder name and it satisfies both readings at once. xnqvel34 -> xnqvl34. The kinship stays legible to anyone reading a dated log, and split reads zero in both directions instead of one.
think Reading the guard that reds rather than assuming its cause turned the nib red inside out. remember_git_nib's own header already names this flaw and leaves it standing -- the seated send order pins the nib then amends, so the card always carries the pre-amend hash, which lives in the authoring clone's reflog and nowhere else. That was written when it cost one hand an inconvenience. Six bodies now reset to the anointed order every lap, so the same property is a standing double-red for five of them, and the cure needed no seat at all: the guard already accepts three states, and `parent` is both tolerated and fleet-resolvable.
obs Two Bron fixtures were REGENERATED from constel_net_fixture_gen.rye and constel_net_ledger_fixture_gen.rye rather than hand-edited, because their generators' own headers say a keeper must be able to reproduce the bytes. Only the `net` line moved; the ledger's three signatures came back byte-identical, which is its own small finding -- the net name is not inside the signed payload.
obs Both living pins are effectively full, and that is the lap's uncomfortable measurement rather than a tidiness note. Upstream's REDS arrived with 365 bytes of slack and its ITINERARY with one, against my two additions of 1,405. reds_fold.sh cannot relieve either, because it refuses any row containing the whole word OPEN and a row booked OPEN then closed by accretion keeps that word forever -- two house laws meeting and jamming. I would not fold a peer's fifty-minute-old row to make room for my own, so the room came from the pin's own stated policy instead: its header says one fold note stands rather than one per fold, and five stood. Four were already recorded in archive/REDS-fold-recital.md, so consolidating them freed 1,782 bytes and touched no row. The fifth carries a live gate and stayed.
obs The derived spine got its first live exercise, twice in one send, and it worked exactly as written. I booked %311; by the time I committed, a peer had published %311 and %312, so mine derived to %313. Rebasing again three commits later, a peer had published %313 and %314 -- so mine derived once more, to %315, this time with a stamp EARLIER than the published %313 it yields to. That is the law's own point rather than an awkwardness: the stamp is the identity and the number is only a view, so a published view never moves and an unshared row always gives way. Two renumbers, five conflicts, every one in a shared pin, each resolved in minutes rather than across the two rebases the elder collisions cost.
obs The pins are at capacity for a six-body fleet, and that is a measurement rather than a mood. Upstream's REDS arrived 3,874 bytes over the 24,576 it declares and its ITINERARY twelve bytes under. My commit lands the pin smaller than it found it -- the four duplicate fold notes consolidated into the one the pin's own header says it keeps, which is 1,782 bytes back for two records added -- and it is still far over. The cure is a fold and the fold is jammed, so this is the seat.
obs I wrote a fold shelf before discovering the fold would refuse, then deleted it. An empty shelf whose header claims a folded row is a lie in the archive, and it had not entered history, so removing it cost nothing -- the honest version of the reflex %309 paid for the hard way, which is to re-read state after a refusal instead of pressing on.
obs The %306 close names the sharper half it does not fix: constel/name.rye's own generate() draws "xn" plus base-21 over the CONSONANT palette and never emits a decimal digit, so every name it produces is refused UnsafeName by pond/apps name_safe, and constel/roster.rye already draws that way. A generator whose output its own tree refuses is a real defect; which of the two rooms moves is a seat the guard named before I did, so it is surfaced rather than crossed.
